import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        int n = scanner.nextInt();
        scanner.nextLine(); // Consume the newline
        String s = scanner.nextLine();
        System.out.println(maxRemovals(s));
    }

    private static int maxRemovals(String s) {
        int count = 0;
        while (true) {
            boolean removed = false;
            for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
                char current = s.charAt(i);
                if (current == 'a') continue; // 'a' has no previous character
                char prevChar = (char) (current - 1);
                boolean canRemove = false;
                if (i > 0 && s.charAt(i - 1) == prevChar) {
                    canRemove = true;
                }
                if (!canRemove && i < s.length() - 1 && s.charAt(i + 1) == prevChar) {
                    canRemove = true;
                }
                if (canRemove) {
                    s = s.substring(0, i) + s.substring(i + 1);
                    count++;
                    removed = true;
                    break;
                }
            }
            if (!removed) break;
        }
        return count;
    }
}
```

### Explanation
1. **Reading Input**: The input consists of the length of the string followed by the string itself.
2. **maxRemovals Function**: This function processes the string to count the maximum number of removable characters.
   - **Loop Until No Removals**: The loop continues until no more characters can be removed in a full pass through the string.
   - **Check Each Character**: For each character, check if it can be removed by examining its adjacent characters.
   - **Remove and Update**: If a character can be removed, update the string and increment the count.
3. **Edge Cases**: The character 'a' is skipped since it has no previous character in the alphabet.

This approach efficiently checks each character in the string and removes valid characters step-by-step, ensuring the maximum number of removals possible according to the given rules.

